 # Lighting, Shadowing & Relighting
 
-Also called: Deferred Surfel Rendering, Split-Sum IBL
 
 ## Overview
-This family extends 3D Gaussian Splatting to support physically based lighting, self-occlusion, and environment reflections. It solves the limitation of standard splatting, which assumes a fixed, uniform lighting environment, by introducing deferred shading pipelines for relighting [4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md] and dedicated light-space rasterization passes for self-shadowing [4487-3d-gaussian-splatting-self-shadowing-t_light.md, 4489-light-space-rasterization-for-t_light.md]. Unlike sibling methods that may use simplified shading functions or voxel-based fallbacks, these approaches provide full PBR material attributes and continuous differentiable gradients for shadows without modifying the primary camera's covariance expansion [4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md, 4487-3d-gaussian-splatting-self-shadowing-t_light.md, 4489-light-space-rasterization-for-t_light.md].
+This family extends 3D Gaussian Splatting to support physically based lighting, self-occlusion, and environment reflections [4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md, 4487-3d-gaussian-splatting-self-shadowing-t_light.md, 4489-light-space-rasterization-for-t_light.md]. It solves the limitation of standard splatting, which assumes a fixed, uniform lighting environment, by introducing deferred shading pipelines for relighting [4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md] and dedicated light-space rasterization passes for self-shadowing [4487-3d-gaussian-splatting-self-shadowing-t_light.md, 4489-light-space-rasterization-for-t_light.md]. Unlike sibling methods that may use simplified shading functions or voxel-based fallbacks, these approaches provide full PBR material attributes and continuous differentiable gradients for shadows without modifying the primary camera's covariance expansion [4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md, 4487-3d-gaussian-splatting-self-shadowing-t_light.md, 4489-light-space-rasterization-for-t_light.md].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate on 3D Gaussian primitives defined by mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}$, covariance $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ (or scales and orientation), opacity $o$, and color $\mathbf{c}$ (or spherical harmonic coefficients). The core challenge is computing the pixel color $C$ and accumulated alpha $\alpha$ given a camera ray. Standard splatting projects 3D Gaussians to 2D ellipses, computes a 2D alpha mask, and blends them in screen space. These models modify the blending or shading stage: some refine the depth metric used for sorting, others integrate the 3D density function directly along the ray, and others use weighted averages to bypass sorting. All assume the Gaussians are sorted or processed in a manner that approximates front-to-back accumulation to ensure correct occlusion.
+Builds on [Shading and Lighting Models](11924-shading-and-lighting-models.md).
 
 For relighting, splats must carry physical material attributes: normal, roughness, reflectance (specular strength), and albedo (diffuse color) [4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md]. These are stored in a parallel Material Buffer aligned with the Splat Buffer [4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md].
